  "slug": "lsst-gravitational-waves-66df",
  "summary": "Four preprints quantify how CO foregrounds, polarization calibration, and cosmic birefringence bias CMB analyses, while DSL 21 cm forecasts target reionization parameters.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is explicit modeling of nuisance signals and instrumental effects that can bias cosmological inference. An analytic halo model for CO and its CIB cross-correlation finds CO adds roughly 2σ bias to cosmological parameters in CMB-HD-like settings. A Simons Observatory study uses simulations to compare methods for transferring absolute polarization-angle calibration between the LAT and SAT. Another paper shows unmodeled anisotropic cosmic birefringence positively biases small-scale CMB lensing bandpowers and uses joint MUSE estimation of lensing and birefringence to address it. The 21 cm item is a forecast rather than a systematics study: Bayesian inference of cosmic dawn and reionization parameters from simulated DSL lunar-orbit global spectra, combined with HERA/SKA-Low data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
